>
  <h2>How shop-and-give apps work</h2>
  <p>
    Most work the same way AmazonSmile did: you shop at partner retailers through an app or browser extension, and a
    percentage of each purchase is donated to a nonprofit you picked — at no extra cost to you, because it comes out
    of the retailer’s marketing budget. The upside is it’s effortless; the downsides are that the percentage is
    usually small, it only works at partner stores, and <strong>you don’t save any money yourself</strong>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>ShopRaise, Givebacks, iGive</strong> — shopping portals/extensions that donate a share of online purchases at national retailers.</li>
    <li><strong>RaiseRight</strong> — buy brand gift cards and a percentage funds your organization.</li>
    <li><strong>Good Circles</strong> — shop <em>local</em> businesses; you save ~10% and a nonprofit you choose gets 10% of the merchant’s profit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The local-first difference</h2>
  <p>
    A shopping-portal app sends a small slice of a national purchase to charity. A local-first model keeps the whole
    transaction close to home: on Good Circles, every <strong>$100</strong> spent locally returns about
    <strong>{dollars(PER_100.shopperSaves)}</strong> to you, sends about <strong>{dollars(PER_100.nonprofit)}</strong>
    to a nonprofit, and keeps roughly <strong>${PER_100.localRecirculation}</strong> circulating in your community —
    versus about <strong>${PER_100.chainRecirculation}</strong> when the same money goes to a national chain.
  </p>
  <p>
    See the full side-by-side on our <a href="/amazonsmile-alternative/">best AmazonSmile alternatives comparison</a>,
    or read <a href="/learn/what-replaced-amazonsmile/">what replaced AmazonSmile</a>.
  </p>
</AnswerPage>
